{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "def find_single_element(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the single element in a sorted array where every other element appears exactly twice.\n\n    Args:\n        nums: A sorted list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        The integer that appears only once in the array.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If the input is not a list.\n        ValueError: If the input list is empty\n    \"\"\"\n\n    if not isinstance(nums, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    if not nums:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty\")\n\n    low = 0\n    high = len(nums) - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if mid % 2 == 0:  # mid is even\n            if mid + 1 < len(nums) and nums[mid] == nums[mid + 1]:\n                low = mid + 2\n            else:\n                high = mid\n        else:  # mid is odd\n            if nums[mid] == nums[mid - 1]:\n                low = mid + 1\n            else:\n                high = mid - 1\n\n    return nums[low]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/120", "solution": "def max_product_tuple(list_of_tuples):\n    if not list_of_tuples or len(list_of_tuples) < 2:\n        return 0\n\n    max_product = 0\n    for i in range(len(list_of_tuples)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(list_of_tuples)):\n            tuple1 = list_of_tuples[i]\n            tuple2 = list_of_tuples[j]\n            product = abs(tuple1[0] * tuple1[1] * tuple2[0] * tuple2[1])\n            max_product = max(max_product, product)\n\n    return max_product"}
